<%%# The show-page activity section, identical on orders, products and customers.
    `note` lets a page add something true about ITS model -- products emit no
    registered action, so theirs are all :change_only -- without any page
    re-deriving how an activity renders. %>
<%% record_type = record.class.name %>
<h2>Activity</h2>

<%% if audit_activity_visible? %>
  <p class="<%= css(:note) %>">
    <%% if local_assigns[:note] %><%%= note %><%% end %>
    One entry per <strong>unit of work</strong>, not one per audit row. Same fidelity as the
    auditor timeline, built from the same value objects.
    <%%= link_to "Full history →", activity_path(record_type: record_type, record_id: record.id) %>
  </p>

  <%%= render "shared/activity_feed", activities: activities %>

  <%% if more %>
    <p class="<%= css(:note_small) %>">
      Showing the <strong><%%= RecordActivity::RECENT_ACTIVITY_LIMIT %></strong> most recent.
      <%%= link_to "See everything →", activity_path(record_type: record_type, record_id: record.id) %>
    </p>
  <%% end %>
<%% else %>
  <p class="<%= css(:note) %>">
    You cannot see this <%%= record_type.downcase %>'s history. The timeline exposes previous
    values and the other records each action touched, so this app gates it — the gem exposes
    everything and leaves that decision to
    <code>RecordActivity#audit_activity_visible?</code>.
  </p>
<%% end %>
